Here is the complete code path for POST /api/owners.

## Routing summary
- POST `/api/owners` is handled by V1 only. `OwnerRestControllerV2` exposes only a GET (`listOwnersPage`). Both controllers are `@RequestMapping("/api")`; the `/owners` sub-path and HTTP method come from the OpenAPI-generated interface `OwnersApi`.
- Order of execution: (1) request-body Bean Validation on `OwnerFieldsDto` via `@Valid`, (2) `@PreAuthorize` role check, (3) controller maps DTO→entity and calls `saveOwner`, (4) service runs its own business validations + normalizations + persistence + audit log, (5) entity→DTO mapping with MapStruct `@AfterMapping` enrichment, plus a `bulkSignupWarning` flag. There are NO `@PrePersist`/`@PostPersist` hooks and NO `ApplicationEventPublisher` usage anywhere in `src/main`.

## 1. The POST mapping + `@Valid` (generated interface)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/api/OwnersApi.java`

```java
@RequestMapping(
    method = RequestMethod.POST,
    value = OwnersApi.PATH_ADD_OWNER,   // "/owners"
    produces = { "application/json" },
    consumes = { "application/json" }
)
default ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(
    @Parameter(name = "OwnerFieldsDto", description = "The pet owner", required = true)
    @Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto
) { ... }
```
The interface is annotated `@Validated`, and the body is `@Valid`, so all Bean Validation constraints on `OwnerFieldsDto` fire before the controller method body runs.

## 2. Controller method
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")
@Override
public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
    HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
    this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
    OwnerDto ownerDto = withBulkSignupWarning(ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner));
    headers.setLocation(UriComponentsBuilder.newInstance()
        .path("/api/owners/{id}").buildAndExpand(owner.getId()).toUri());
    return new ResponseEntity<>(ownerDto, headers, HttpStatus.CREATED);
}

private OwnerDto withBulkSignupWarning(OwnerDto ownerDto) {
    ownerDto.setBulkSignupWarning(this.clinicService.isBulkSignupWarningActive());
    return ownerDto;
}
```

## 3. Request DTO with validation annotations
`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/dto/OwnerFieldsDto.java` (constraints are on the getters):

- `firstName`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\p{L}]+([ '-][\p{L}]+){0,2}$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `lastName`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\p{L}]+([ '-][\p{L}]+){0,2}\.?$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `address`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "(?s).*\S.*") @Size(min = 1, max = 255)`
- `city`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "(?s).*\S.*") @Size(min = 1, max = 80)`
- `telephone`: field carries `@org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.validation.TelephoneValidation`; getter carries `@NotNull @Size(min = 1, max = 20)`
- `email`: `@Size(min = 3, max = 255) @jakarta.validation.constraints.Email` (optional)
- `registrationDate`: `@Valid` `LocalDate` (optional; no future-date constraint here — that is enforced in the service)
- `birthDate`: `@Valid` `LocalDate` (optional)
- `postcode`: `@Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{4}$")` (optional)
- `sharesHousehold`: `Boolean`, default `false`, no constraint (write-only flag that bypasses the household-duplicate block)

Note: the persistent entity `Owner` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`) carries its own JPA/validation annotations (`@NotEmpty` on address/city/telephone, `@Pattern` E.164 on telephone, `@Email` on email), but on the POST path the controller maps from `OwnerFieldsDto`, so it is the DTO constraints above that gate the request.

## 4. The custom telephone validator (request-time side-effect)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneValidation.java` — the constraint annotation, `@Constraint(validatedBy = TelephoneValidator.class)`, `@Target(FIELD)`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneValidator.java`:
```java
public boolean isValid(String telephone, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
    return E164Telephone.toE164(telephone) != null;
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/E164Telephone.java` holds the normalization logic (`toE164`) used by BOTH the validator and the service normalization, so validation and stored value agree. Country-code national-length rules: `+61`→9 digits, `+1`→10; overall 8–15 digits after `+`.

## 5. Service method `saveOwner` and its ordered side-effects
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`

```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void saveOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    assertPostcodeValid(owner);                 // throws InvalidPostcodeException (region range check)
    assertRegistrationDateNotFuture(owner);     // throws FutureRegistrationDateException
    if (owner.getTelephone() != null) {
        owner.setTelephone(normalizeTelephone(owner.getTelephone()));  // E164Telephone.toE164
    }
    if (owner.getEmail() != null) {
        owner.setEmail(normalizeEmail(owner.getEmail()));              // lower-case
    }
    assertEmailDomainAllowed(owner);            // throws DisposableEmailDomainException
    if (owner.getAddress() != null) {
        owner.setAddress(normalizeAddress(owner.getAddress()));        // trim/collapse/upper/expand ST,RD,AVE
    }
    LocalDate effectiveDate = owner.getRegistrationDate() != null ? owner.getRegistrationDate() : LocalDate.now();
    owner.setRegistrationDate(rollToBusinessDay(effectiveDate));       // weekend -> Monday
    if (owner.getCustomerCode() == null) {
        owner.setCustomerCode(generateCustomerCode(owner));            // "<REGION>-<HASH8>" over tel+lastName
    }
    boolean creating = owner.isNew();
    if (creating) {
        owner.setNamesakeCount(countNamesakes(owner));                 // full-table scan
        assertDailyCapacity(owner);             // throws DailyOwnerLimitException (>=100/day)
        assertCityCapacity(owner);              // throws CityOwnerLimitException (>=50/city)
    }
    assignHouseholdId(owner);                    // SHA-256(lastName|postcode)[0:12], or null
    assertIdentityUnique(owner);                 // throws DuplicateIdentityException (identityKey = tel|email|householdId)
    if (creating) {
        assertHouseholdUnique(owner);            // throws HouseholdDuplicateException unless sharesHousehold
    }
    ownerRepository.save(owner);                 // JPA/JDBC persist
    populateHouseholdSize(owner);                // transient householdSize for presentation
    if (creating) {
        AUDIT.info("owner created: id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={} membershipLevel={}", ...);  // audit log side-effect
    }
}
```

Exact create-path ordering: postcode range check → future-date check → telephone normalize → email normalize → disposable-email-domain check → address normalize → registrationDate defaulting + business-day roll → customerCode generation → namesake count → daily-capacity check → city-capacity check → householdId assignment → identity-uniqueness check → household-uniqueness check → `ownerRepository.save` → householdSize populate → AUDIT log. `saveOwner` is `@Transactional`, so any thrown business exception rolls back the whole thing.

The helper methods and constants (`CITY_OWNER_LIMIT=50`, `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT=100`, `BULK_SIGNUP_THRESHOLD=80`, `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS`, `normalizeAddress`, `generateCustomerCode`, `assignHouseholdId`, `assertIdentityUnique`, `assertHouseholdUnique`, `isBulkSignupWarningActive`) are all in the same file (quoted above).

Business-rule exception classes (thrown from the above), same package `service`:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/DailyOwnerLimitException.java` (→ HTTP 429 per Javadoc)
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/CityOwnerLimitException.java` (→ HTTP 409)
- Plus `InvalidPostcodeException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException`, `DisposableEmailDomainException`, `DuplicateIdentityException`, `HouseholdDuplicateException` (referenced in the same package; not quoted).

## 6. Mapper side-effects (entity → response DTO)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — MapStruct interface. `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` ignores `id` and `pets`. `toOwnerDto(Owner)` runs a chain of `@AfterMapping` enrichers that add derived, presentation-only fields: `displayName` ("Last, First"), `initials`, `checkDigit` (Luhn over customerCode), `membershipNumber`, `membershipPoints`/`membershipLevel`, `contactPreference` (EMAIL/PHONE), `locality` (region from customerCode), `ageBand`. The controller then overlays `bulkSignupWarning` via `withBulkSignupWarning`.

Derived-value logic those mappers call lives on the entity `Owner`: `getIdentityKey`, `getCheckDigit`, `getMembershipPoints`/`getMembershipLevel`, `getAgeBand`, `getTelephoneDisplay` (all quoted in the `Owner.java` read above).

No file modifications were made.

